<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Autonomous agent dispatcher: runs `dev agent tick` every 30 seconds.

  A LaunchAgent, NOT a LaunchDaemon. A LaunchDaemon runs as root at boot with no
  user session: no keychain (so no `gh` auth), no Docker Desktop, no nvm `node`
  for `claude`. Everything this needs lives in the user session.

  Install:   cp launchd/com.bryzek.dev-agent.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
             launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.bryzek.dev-agent.plist
  Uninstall: launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/com.bryzek.dev-agent
  Verify:    dev agent tick --dry-run     (prints every decision, executes none)

  /bin/zsh -lc supplies the interactive PATH (homebrew, nvm's node for `claude`,
  sbt, gh); launchd's own environment is nearly empty.

  NO LOCKING AND NO LOGGING IN THIS FILE — both live inside `dev agent tick`.
  The tick has two phases needing two different locks (vitals must never sit
  behind the work lock), so a single wrapper here would gate heartbeats behind a
  slow clone and fake an outage on a healthy machine. And note that the usual
  `/usr/bin/flock -n` idiom does not exist on macOS: flock(1) ships with
  util-linux and is in neither the base system nor homebrew, so a plist using it
  would fail with "command not found" on every fire while launchd recorded only a
  non-zero exit. The tick uses Ruby's File#flock instead. Logging is likewise the
  command's job so every path is defined in one place
  (~/Library/Logs/dev-agent/).

  HOST PREREQUISITES — these are what silently kill an unattended machine:

    Automatic login enabled
      A LaunchAgent runs only in a logged-in GUI session. After a reboot without
      auto-login the dispatcher is silently dead, and the queue looks calm either
      way.

    FileVault stays ON
      It defeats auto-login, since the disk needs a password at boot. A
      deliberate tradeoff on a box holding `gh` credentials and the git-crypt'd
      env repo: use `sudo fdesetup authrestart` for planned reboots and let the
      runner-offline alert catch the unplanned case.

    Sleep disabled       sudo pmset -a sleep 0 disablesleep 1
      A sleeping machine claims nothing.

    Docker Desktop starts at login
      `claude-db` needs it.

    `gh` authenticated, `dev auth ai` token stored
      Attribution to the AI, and it bootstraps self-registration.

  Not on that list: registering the machine, setting its concurrency, or editing
  any fleet config. The first tick does all of that itself.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.bryzek.dev-agent</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/zsh</string>
    <string>-lc</string>
    <string>~/code/devops/bin/dev agent tick</string>
  </array>
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>30</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
